{"id":122,"date":"2026-07-09T14:28:15","date_gmt":"2026-07-09T14:28:15","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/localhost:8181\/straight-answers-on-replacing-hvac-in-a-doylestown-borough-home\/"},"modified":"2026-07-15T21:01:27","modified_gmt":"2026-07-15T21:01:27","slug":"straight-answers-on-replacing-hvac-in-a-doylestown-borough-home","status":"publish","type":"page","link":"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/straight-answers-on-replacing-hvac-in-a-doylestown-borough-home\/","title":{"rendered":"Your Doylestown Stone Farmhouse Stores Heat \u2014 Your Furnace Ignores It"},"content":{"rendered":"\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--dark-gradient has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-cover alignfull s-hero-cover\" style=\"padding-top:3rem;padding-right:2rem;padding-bottom:3rem;padding-left:2rem\"><span aria-hidden=\"true\" class=\"wp-block-cover__background has-contrast-background-color has-background-dim-70 has-background-dim\"><\/span><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"1024\" class=\"wp-block-cover__image-background wp-image-1790\" alt=\"winter - professional HVAC service photo\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-winter-1.png\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-winter-1.png 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-winter-1-300x300.png 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-winter-1-150x150.png 150w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/ez2-winter-1-768x768.png 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><div class=\"wp-block-cover__inner-container has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-cover-is-layout-ff0c90b8 wp-block-cover-is-layout-constrained\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center has-base-color has-text-color s-hero-cover__title wp-block-heading\">Stone Walls Demand Different Heat<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"has-text-align-center s-lead has-base-color has-text-color has-medium-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">Stone farmhouses in Bucks County weren&#039;t built with HVAC in mind\u2014they were built with walls thick enough to store heat like a battery.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-buttons is-content-justification-center is-layout-flex wp-container-core-buttons-is-layout-35f06ea7 wp-block-buttons-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"\/contact\/\">Get Your Numbers<\/a><\/div>\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-outline is-style-outline--1\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"#content\">Learn More<\/a><\/div>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"s-trust-bar\"><div class=\"s-trust__item\">15+ Years Serving PA<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Licensed &amp; Insured<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Same-Day Emergency<\/div><div class=\"s-trust__item\">Local to Your Area<\/div><\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--ink has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Why Your Thermostat Lies<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Forced-air heating works by warming the air in a room, and in a well-insulated modern home with drywall and fiberglass batt insulation, that heated air stays warm long enough to keep you comfortable. In a stone farmhouse, the air temperature might read 70 degrees on the thermostat, but the walls, floor, and ceiling are still 60 degrees, and your body loses heat to those cold surfaces through radiation.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">This is called mean radiant temperature, and it&#039;s the reason you feel cold in a stone house even when the air is technically warm. The problem is compounded by the way forced-air systems cycle on and off. When the thermostat calls for heat, the furnace kicks on and blows warm air until the air temperature reaches the setpoint, then shuts off.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In a stone house, that might happen in 10 or 15 minutes, but the walls haven&#039;t warmed up yet, so as soon as the system shuts off, the cold stone pulls heat out of the air and the room feels cold again. Some people try to fix this by turning up the thermostat, which just wastes energy without improving comfort.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Others install a larger furnace, thinking they need more capacity, but that makes the cycling problem worse. The real solution is to either switch to a radiant heating system that warms the surfaces directly, or to modify the forced-air system so it runs at lower temperatures for longer periods, giving the stone time to absorb and store heat.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">High-efficiency furnaces and heat pumps with variable-speed blowers can deliver continuous low-volume airflow that gradually warms the thermal mass without short-cycling. Register placement also matters: floor registers are better than ceiling registers in a stone house because warm air rising off the floor eventually warms the walls as it circulates.<\/p>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--ink has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-stats is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">18-24\u2033<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Foundation wall thickness in original farmhouses<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">10-15\u00b0<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Difference between air temp and wall surface temp<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">110\u00b0F<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Water temperature needed for radiant heat in thermal mass<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-stats__item is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<p class=\"s-stats__number has-x-large-font-size wp-block-paragraph\">R-3<\/p>\n\n<p class=\"s-stats__label wp-block-paragraph\">Insulation value of uninsulated stone walls<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text\" style=\"grid-template-columns:50% auto\"><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/363044088_2d14d4cea7_b.jpg\" alt=\"furnace basement heating\" class=\"wp-image-179\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/363044088_2d14d4cea7_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/363044088_2d14d4cea7_b-300x200.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/363044088_2d14d4cea7_b-768x512.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;Starting up again (1 of 4)&quot; by Unhindered by Talent, BY-SA, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Radiant Heat and Thermal Mass<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Radiant heating is the natural complement to thermal mass construction, and it&#039;s increasingly common in high-end restorations of Doylestown-area stone farmhouses. The concept is simple: instead of heating the air, you heat the floors or walls directly, and those surfaces then radiate warmth to the rest of the room.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In a home with thick stone walls and wide-plank floors, radiant heat feels like what the house was always meant to have\u2014gentle, even, silent, and deeply comfortable. The stone absorbs the heat and holds it for hours, which means the system doesn&#039;t have to run continuously to maintain comfort.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A properly designed radiant system in a stone house can maintain a 70-degree indoor temperature with water temperatures as low as 110 degrees, which is perfect for high-efficiency condensing boilers and heat pumps. The challenge is installation.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Radiant floor heat requires either tearing up the existing floors to embed PEX tubing in the subfloor, or installing a new subfloor on top of the old one, which raises the floor height and creates transitions at doorways. Some contractors install radiant panels under existing flooring by accessing the joists from below, but this only works if you have an accessible basement or crawl space. The other consideration is cooling.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Radiant floors can&#039;t provide air conditioning, so if you want whole-house cooling, you need a separate system. Some homeowners install a ducted AC system or mini-splits for summer and run the radiant heat in winter, treating them as separate seasonal systems.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The most practical setup for a Doylestown stone farmhouse is usually radiant floor heat for the main living areas, supplemented by mini-splits or a high-velocity ducted system for cooling and for heating the upstairs bedrooms, which are often wood-framed additions that don&#039;t have the same thermal mass as the stone core of the house.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--dark-gradient has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Stone Farmhouse Services<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-columns alignwide s-cards-3 is-layout-flex wp-container-core-columns-is-layout-794e3cfa wp-block-columns-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-card--accent is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Radiant System Design<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Floor and wall heating retrofit planning for high-mass buildings.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-card--accent is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Thermal Load Analysis<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Understanding your stone walls&#039; effect on furnace sizing and efficiency.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-card--accent is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Zone Installation<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Multi-level homes need separate thermostats\u2014we set them up right.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-card--accent is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Moisture &amp; Insulation<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Interior insulation strategy that doesn&#039;t trap moisture in stone.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-card--accent is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Mini-Split Systems<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Quiet, efficient heads for individual rooms without extensive ductwork.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-column s-card s-card--accent is-layout-flow wp-block-column-is-layout-flow\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Emergency Service<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Same-day response when your stone house heater fails in winter.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-media-text has-media-on-the-right\" style=\"grid-template-columns:auto 50%\"><div class=\"wp-block-media-text__content\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Insulation Without Trapping Moisture<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Stone walls are beautiful and durable, but they&#039;re not insulated, and they let moisture pass through. Traditional lime mortar is vapor-permeable, which means water vapor can move from inside the house to outside, or vice versa, depending on the temperature and humidity gradient. This is actually a good thing\u2014it lets the wall dry out if it gets wet from a roof leak or wind-driven rain.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">But it also means the wall is a thermal bridge, conducting heat directly from the warm interior to the cold exterior in winter. If you want to improve energy performance, you need to insulate the walls, but you have to do it in a way that doesn&#039;t trap moisture and cause the stone or the interior framing to rot.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Interior insulation is the most common approach because it doesn&#039;t alter the exterior appearance of the building. The challenge is that adding insulation to the interior surface of a stone wall changes the wall&#039;s moisture dynamics. The stone is now on the cold side of the insulation, which means it stays colder and takes longer to dry if it gets wet.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">The safer approach is to use vapor-permeable insulation like mineral wool batts or dense-pack cellulose, with an interior finish that allows some vapor transmission. You also need to leave a small air gap between the insulation and the stone to allow any moisture that does accumulate to drain down and evaporate.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Some high-performance retrofits use a hybrid approach: interior insulation on the upper floors where the walls are thinner and easier to work with, and exterior insulation on the foundation or basement walls where the stone is thickest and where moisture is already a concern.<\/p>\n\n<\/div><figure class=\"wp-block-media-text__media\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"800\" height=\"600\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation.jpg\" alt=\"mini split heat pump\" class=\"wp-image-202\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation.jpg 800w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/Ductless_Mini_Split_Heat_Pump_Installation-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;Ductless Mini Split Heat Pump Installation&quot; by Vernon Air Con, BY-SA, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure><\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--sand has-tint-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Zoning Strategy for Multi-Level Homes<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-steps\">\n<li>Assess the building sections \u2014 Original stone core, wood-framed additions, basement, attic\u2014each zone has different thermal behavior and load requirements.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Divide into temperature zones \u2014 First floor vs. second floor, main house vs. addition, occupied vs. storage spaces\u2014allow independent thermostat control.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Install zoning dampers or mini-splits \u2014 Dampers automatically adjust airflow based on which zones call for heat; mini-splits put individual heads where you need them most.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Use setback scheduling \u2014 Lower the thermostat at night and let thermal mass release stored heat; bring the system back up gradually in the morning.<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Monitor comfort and adjust \u2014 Stone houses need patience\u2014allow 2\u20133 weeks for the building to stabilize at new settings before making additional changes.<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Attic Insulation and Ventilation<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-indent has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Roof insulation depends entirely on whether the attic is conditioned space. If the attic is unfinished and used only for storage, insulate the attic floor\u2014the ceiling of the rooms below\u2014and leave the attic itself unconditioned. This is the most cost-effective strategy and it aligns with how these homes were originally designed.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You can blow in cellulose or fiberglass to R-40 or R-60, air-seal the penetrations, and call it done. If the attic is finished as living space, you need to insulate the roof deck itself, turning the attic into conditioned space. Many stone farmhouses have slate roofs that are original to the building and expected to last another century with proper maintenance.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If that&#039;s your situation, think twice before cutting into the roof structure. A better approach is to insulate the attic floor if possible, or to use a vented assembly if you must insulate the roof.<\/p>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--dark-gradient has-contrast-background-color has-background has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list s-pills s-pills--glass\">\n<li>Stone farmhouse walls: R-value ~3, need interior insulation before equipment sizing<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Geothermal: Bucks County shale requires horizontal loops with sufficient acreage<\/li>\n\n\n<li>18th-century timber frames: structural assessment required before ceiling-mount installation<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Dual-fuel breakeven: heat pump wins above 30\u00b0F, propane backup below in this climate<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-diagnostic has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-b267b866 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:3rem;padding-right:3rem;padding-bottom:3rem;padding-left:3rem\">\n<h3 class=\"wp-block-heading\">Stone Farmhouse Diagnostic<\/h3>\n\n\n\n<ul class=\"wp-block-list\">\n<li>Interior walls damp or cold to touch in winter \u2014 thermal bridging through masonry<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Musty smell in below-grade rooms \u2014 moisture wicking through stone without vapor management<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Existing HVAC ducts running through uninsulated crawlspace \u2014 losing 30\u201340% of output<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Multiple additions at different levels \u2014 each section needs independent load calculation<\/li>\n\n\n<li>Wood structural elements showing signs of moisture \u2014 ventilation strategy needed before HVAC sizing<\/li>\n\n<\/ul>\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group alignfull s-band s-band--cream has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-08713647 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:6rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:6rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">HVAC Questions for Stone Farmhouses<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group s-accordion s-accordion--bordered has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-1583283a wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\">\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Why does my stone house feel colder than the thermostat reading?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">You&#039;re feeling the mean radiant temperature, which is the average temperature of all the surfaces around you\u2014walls, floor, ceiling. In a stone house with thick, cold walls, those surfaces can be 10 or 15 degrees colder than the air, and your body radiates heat to them, making you feel cold even when the air temperature is comfortable. The solution is to heat the surfaces, not just the air.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Radiant floor or wall heating is ideal, but you can also improve comfort with a forced-air system by running it at lower temperatures for longer periods, which gives the stone time to warm up and store heat.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Is radiant floor heat worth the cost in a stone farmhouse?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">If you&#039;re already doing a major renovation that involves tearing up floors, yes. Radiant heat works beautifully with thermal mass and provides a level of comfort that forced air can&#039;t match. If you&#039;re not renovating, the cost of retrofitting radiant systems into an existing floor is usually hard to justify unless you have easy access from below.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">In that case, a well-designed mini-split system or a high-efficiency forced-air system with a variable-speed blower can deliver good comfort at a fraction of the cost.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Can I insulate my stone walls from the inside without causing moisture problems?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Yes, but it has to be done carefully. Use vapor-permeable insulation like mineral wool or dense-pack cellulose, leave a small air gap between the insulation and the stone, and make sure your interior humidity levels are controlled. Avoid closed-cell spray foam directly against the stone, as it can trap moisture and lead to deterioration. If you&#039;re unsure, consult with a building scientist or an architect who specializes in historic retrofits.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Stone wall insulation is one area where doing it wrong can cause expensive long-term damage, so it&#039;s worth getting expert advice.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n\n<details class=\"wp-block-details is-layout-flow wp-block-details-is-layout-flow\"><summary>Should I use a bigger furnace to heat my stone house faster?<\/summary>\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">No. Oversizing a furnace in a stone house makes the cycling problem worse. The furnace will satisfy the thermostat faster, short-cycle more frequently, and waste energy without improving comfort. The stone doesn&#039;t have time to absorb and store heat if the system is constantly turning on and off.<\/p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">A properly sized high-efficiency furnace with a variable-speed blower, or a heat pump, is a much better choice because it can deliver continuous low-volume airflow and let the thermal mass do its job.<\/p>\n\n<\/details>\n\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n<\/div>\n\n\n\n<div class=\"wp-block-group has-global-padding is-layout-constrained wp-container-core-group-is-layout-633910b2 wp-block-group-is-layout-constrained\" style=\"padding-top:4.5rem;padding-right:2.5rem;padding-bottom:4.5rem;padding-left:2.5rem\">\n\n<h2 class=\"wp-element-heading has-text-align-center wp-block-heading\">Our Work in Your Neighborhood<\/h2>\n\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-gallery alignwide has-nested-images columns-3 is-cropped s-gallery wp-block-gallery-2 is-layout-flex wp-block-gallery-is-layout-flex\">\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"768\" data-id=\"188\" src=\"\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b.jpg\" alt=\"hvac technician repair\" class=\"wp-image-188\" srcset=\"http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b.jpg 1024w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b-300x225.jpg 300w, http:\/\/127.0.0.1:8181\/wp-content\/uploads\/2026\/07\/51282276312_f3389b56f2_b-768x576.jpg 768w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px\" \/><span class=\"s-img-badge s-img-badge--stock\" title=\"&quot;AC Service&quot; by SuperTech HVAC Services, BY-SA, via Openverse\">Stock<\/span><\/figure>\n\n\n<figure class=\"wp-block-image size-large\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"1024\" height=\"979\" data-id=\"196\"